 that after you  got the Nobel Peace Prize we have a  choice we still have a choice and I'm  gonna say now we still have the choice  nonviolent coexistence of violent core  knowledge and it's right before our eyes  are we gonna choose the pathway of those  who've been on the streets of America  for the past six seven eight years  saying no justice no peace are we gonna  go down the pathway of those and then be  intent on destroying America which is a  pathway we kind of set up you know as  daddy said America being the greatest  purveyor of violence and so hey it's the  forest  I choose non-violence I continue in that  work I still believe in it I know in the  end that if we're gonna get to permanent  peace which means justice that  non-violence has to be the way to know  how he died hey the only thing that I  can do is you told a very poignant story  about watching George Floyd's daughter  and what you understood about her  situation I was five and my father was  assassinated brutally she's six so we're  the same age and immediately when this  happened and I heard that he had a six  year old daughter it took me back to  when my father was brutally taken from  us  and I just I I know the pain that comes  from that  losing the daddy it's such a young age  and the things that she's gonna have to  wrestle with in particular the anger and  and having to guard against hating my  mother used to always tell us in our  household we're not to hate the man of  the person who killed your father we're  not gonna hate the man and she said that  over and over again  and yet I went on a journey where I did  hate for a while I hated white people  and especially white males and that  journey was you in my 20s and into my  early 30s most people didn't know it  because of the kind of household I grew  up in but I was dealing with that and  the anger inside and the rage inside  we gotta change America I mean she said  it to us  my daddy changed the world she spoke  prophetically out of 